{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat src/jolt/dao/posix/file.jolt; cat src/cljc/dao/space/store/fs.cljc'","aggregated_output":"(ns dao.posix.file\n  \"Jolt-only POSIX descriptor plumbing for the jolt branch of dao.jing.file's\n   file seams (J4a, collab/1792769713135-architect-j4-file-seams-design §2):\n   descriptor I/O over native/jolt-file, which `bb build:jolt-native` builds.\n\n   dao.posix.* is not the dao.host profile (src/jolt/README.md): it has no\n   src/cljc counterpart, no portable entries and no refusal table, and it is\n   reached only from :jolt arms, never from a :default arm. It is linkage behind\n   dao.jing.file's existing seams (open-file!, file-length!, read-int!,\n   read-bytes!, truncate-file!, append-frame!, sync-file!, close-file!), which\n   call it from their :jolt arms. The framing, validation, recovery cut and\n   replay stay in dao.jing.file; nothing here decides what a frame is or where\n   to truncate.\n\n   A handle owns one descriptor, opened read/write with close-on-exec, never\n   truncated and never O_APPEND. Reads and writes are positional (pread,\n   pwrite), so no seek position is shared. The read and write loops live here:\n   short transfers continue, EINTR retries, and a read that meets end of file\n   before its count, or a write that makes no progress, throws: no zero\n   padding and no silent short frame. The frame's signed 32-bit big-endian\n   length prefix is packed and unpacked by the wrapper (review F2).\n\n   Every native buffer is caller-owned, its release established as soon as\n   it is acquired (`with-native`), and every bytes argument is copied in and\n   every result copied out, so no caller array is ever shared with C. Native\n   entries that can wait on the disk are bound :blocking, so a collection\n   does not wait for them.\n\n   Loading this namespace is the library gate: it refuses, by throwing, when\n   the wrapper is missing or answers another layout, before any file is\n   touched.\"\n  (:require\n    [clojure.string :as str]\n    [dao.host.text :as text]\n    [jolt.ffi :as ffi]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The native library\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def ^:private platform\n  ;; as dao.host.hash decides it\n  (let [os (str/lower-case (or (System/getProperty \"os.name\") \"\"))\n        arch (System/getProperty \"os.arch\")]\n    (str (if (str/includes? os \"mac\") \"darwin\" \"linux\") \"-\"\n         (if (#{\"aarch64\" \"arm64\"} arch) \"arm64\" \"x86_64\"))))\n\n\n(def ^:private library\n  (str (System/getProperty \"user.dir\") \"/build/native/\" platform\n       \"/jolt-file/lib/libjolt_file\"\n       (if (str/starts-with? platform \"darwin\") \".dylib\" \".so\")))\n\n\n(defn- refuse-load\n  [message data]\n  (throw (ex-info (str \"jolt-file refused: \" message)\n                  (assoc data :library library))))\n\n\n(when-not (.exists (java.io.File. ^String library))\n  (refuse-load \"the wrapper is not built (bb build:jolt-native)\" {}))\n\n\n(ffi/load-library library)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-layout-version \"jolt_file_layout_version\" [] :int32)\n\n\n;; native/jolt-file/jolt_file.c JOLT_FILE_LAYOUT (3 since J4d: an older\n;; library is refused here, not at the first key-file write)\n(def ^:private layout 3)\n\n\n(let [actual (c-layout-version)]\n  (when-not (= layout actual)\n    (refuse-load \"the library writes another layout\"\n                 {:expected layout, :actual actual})))\n\n\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-eintr \"jolt_file_eintr\" [] :int32)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-make-parents \"jolt_file_make_parents\"\n  [:pointer :int64] :int64 :blocking)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-open \"jolt_file_open\" [:pointer :int64] :int64 :blocking)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-length \"jolt_file_length\" [:int64] :int64 :blocking)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-pread \"jolt_file_pread\"\n  [:int64 :int64 :pointer :int64 :int64] :int64 :blocking)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-pwrite \"jolt_file_pwrite\"\n  [:int64 :int64 :pointer :int64 :int64] :int64 :blocking)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-truncate \"jolt_file_truncate\" [:int64 :int64] :int64 :blocking)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-sync \"jolt_file_sync\" [:int64] :int64 :blocking)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-close \"jolt_file_close\" [:int64] :int64 :blocking)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-s32be-get \"jolt_file_s32be_get\" [:pointer :int64] :int32)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-s32be-put \"jolt_file_s32be_put\" [:pointer :int64 :int32] :void)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-strerror \"jolt_file_strerror\" [:int64 :pointer :int64] :int64)\n;; J4b (layout 2)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-classify \"jolt_file_classify\"\n  [:pointer :int64 :int32] :int64 :blocking)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-fclassify \"jolt_file_fclassify\" [:int64] :int64 :blocking)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-fidentity \"jolt_file_fidentity\" [:int64 :pointer] :int64 :blocking)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-open-excl \"jolt_file_open_excl\" [:pointer :int64] :int64 :blocking)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-open-read \"jolt_file_open_read\" [:pointer :int64] :int64 :blocking)\n;; J4d (layout 3)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-open-private \"jolt_file_open_private\" [:pointer :int64] :int64 :blocking)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-eexist \"jolt_file_eexist\" [] :int32)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-lock \"jolt_file_lock\" [:int64] :int64 :blocking)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-realpath \"jolt_file_realpath\"\n  [:pointer :int64 :pointer :int64] :int64 :blocking)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-rename \"jolt_file_rename\"\n  [:pointer :int64 :pointer :int64] :int64 :blocking)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-sync-dir \"jolt_file_sync_dir\" [:pointer :int64] :int64 :blocking)\n\n\n(def ^:private eintr (c-eintr))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Native memory and errors\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- with-native\n  \"(f p) over caller-owned native memory `p` of `n` bytes (one for an empty\n   request), freed however `f` exits. The release is established before\n   anything else can fail.\"\n  [n f]\n  (let [p (ffi/alloc (max 1 n))]\n    ;; a null answer acquired nothing, so it is refused before the release\n    ;; is established, never handed to ffi/free (J4a review R4)\n    (when (ffi/null? p)\n      (throw (ex-info \"jolt-file native allocation failed\" {:bytes n})))\n    (try\n      (f p)\n      (finally (ffi/free p)))))\n\n\n(defn- error-text\n  [errno]\n  (with-native 256\n    (fn [buf]\n      (let [n (c-strerror errno buf 256)]\n        (if (pos? n) (ffi/read-bytes buf n) (str \"errno \" errno))))))\n\n\n(defn- io-error\n  \"The host exception for a failed operation: the operation, the path, the\n   errno this invocation answered and its text, plus any offset context.\"\n  [handle operation errno data]\n  (ex-info (str \"dao.jing.file \" operation \" failed on \" (:path handle) \": \"\n                (error-text errno) \" (errno \" errno \")\")\n           (merge {:operation operation, :path (:path handle), :errno errno} data)))\n\n\n(defn- path-bytes!\n  \"The UTF-8 path bytes copied into native memory, (f p n).\"\n  [path f]\n  (let [bs (text/utf8-encode path)\n        n (alength bs)]\n    (with-native n\n      (fn [p]\n        (when (pos? n) (ffi/write-array p bs))\n        (f p n)))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Single transfers: the test seam\n;; =============================================================================\n\n;; One system call each, answering a count or -errno. The loops below call\n;; them through these vars, which is the test-only indirection the J4a gate\n;; redefines to inject short, EINTR, zero-progress and failing transfers.\n;; There is no production fault mode.\n\n(defn- pread-once\n  [fd offset buf at n]\n  (c-pread fd offset buf at n))\n\n\n(defn- pwrite-once\n  [fd offset buf at n]\n  (c-pwrite fd offset buf at n))\n\n\n(defn- fsync-once\n  [fd]\n  (c-sync fd))\n\n\n(defn- close-once\n  [fd]\n  (c-close fd))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The handle\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- fd-of\n  [handle]\n  (when @(:released handle)\n    (throw (ex-info (str \"dao.jing.file descriptor already closed: \" (:path handle))\n                    {:path (:path handle)})))\n  (:fd handle))\n\n\n(defn open\n  \"Open or create the file at path for positional reading and writing,\n   creating its missing parent directories (checked: a component that cannot\n   be a directory fails). Never truncates. Answers the handle.\n\n   A relative path resolves against user.dir, as java.io.File does: the jolt\n   launcher runs the process from its own root and passes the caller's\n   working directory as user.dir, so the process cwd is not it.\"\n  [path]\n  (let [native-path (.getAbsolutePath (java.io.File. ^String path))\n        made (path-bytes! native-path c-make-parents)]\n    (when (neg? made)\n      (throw (io-error {:path path} \"create-parents\" (- made) {})))\n    (let [fd (path-bytes! native-path c-open)]\n      (when (neg? fd)\n        (throw (io-error {:path path} \"open\" (- fd) {})))\n      ;; :failed is the content log's fail-closed state (review F3): nil, or\n      ;; the operation whose failure left the file's tail uncertain\n      {:fd fd, :path path, :released (atom false), :failed (atom nil)})))\n\n\n(defn length\n  \"The file's size by fstat.\"\n  [handle]\n  (let [n (c-length (fd-of handle))]\n    (when (neg? n) (throw (io-error handle \"fstat\" (- n) {})))\n    n))\n\n\n(defn- read-into!\n  \"Fill buf[0, n) from file offset `offset`: short reads continue, EINTR\n   retries, end of file before n bytes throws.\"\n  [handle buf offset n]\n  (let [fd (fd-of handle)]\n    (loop [done 0]\n      (when (< done n)\n        (let [r (pread-once fd (+ offset done) buf done (- n done))]\n          (cond\n            (pos? r) (recur (+ done r))\n            (zero? r) (throw (ex-info (str \"dao.jing.file pread met end of file on \"\n                                           (:path handle) \" before a complete read\")\n                                      {:operation \"pread\", :path (:path handle),\n                                       :offset offset, :wanted n, :read done}))\n            (= r (- eintr)) (recur done)\n            :else (throw (io-error handle \"pread\" (- r)\n                                   {:offset offset, :wanted n, :read done}))))))))\n\n\n(defn read-s32be\n  \"The signed 32-bit big-endian integer at offset, converted by the wrapper\n   (review F2): a prefix with the top bit set is negative.\"\n  [handle offset]\n  (with-native 4\n    (fn [buf]\n      (read-into! handle buf offset 4)\n      (c-s32be-get buf 0))))\n\n\n(defn read-bytes\n  \"The n bytes at offset, copied into a fresh byte array.\"\n  [handle offset n]\n  (if (zero? n)\n    (byte-array 0)\n    (with-native n\n      (fn [buf]\n        (read-into! handle buf offset n)\n        (ffi/read-array buf n)))))\n\n\n(defn truncate!\n  \"ftruncate to len. dao.jing.file calls it only at its validated recovery\n   cut; nothing here truncates on a failed read.\"\n  [handle len]\n  (let [r (c-truncate (fd-of handle) len)]\n    (when (neg? r) (throw (io-error handle \"ftruncate\" (- r) {:length len})))\n    nil))\n\n\n(defn- write-from!\n  \"Write buf[0, n) at file offset `offset`: short writes continue, EINTR\n   retries, zero progress or an error throws.\"\n  [handle buf offset n]\n  (let [fd (fd-of handle)]\n    (loop [done 0]\n      (when (< done n)\n        (let [r (pwrite-once fd (+ offset done) buf done (- n done))]\n          (cond\n            (pos? r) (recur (+ done r))\n            (= r (- eintr)) (recur done)\n            (zero? r) (throw (ex-info (str \"dao.jing.file pwrite made no progress on \"\n                                           (:path handle))\n                                      {:operation \"pwrite\", :path (:path handle),\n                                       :offset offset, :wanted n, :written done}))\n            :else (throw (io-error handle \"pwrite\" (- r)\n                                   {:offset offset, :wanted n, :written done}))))))))\n\n\n(defn- refuse-if-failed!\n  \"Throw when this handle is fail-closed (review F3): a previous append or\n   fsync failed, so the end of the file is uncertain and nothing more may be\n   appended through this descriptor until it is closed and the file reopened\n   (where dao.jing.file's validated recovery decides what stands).\"\n  [handle]\n  (when-let [operation (some-> (:failed handle) deref)]\n    (throw (ex-info (str \"dao.jing.file \" (:path handle) \" refuses further appends: a \"\n                         \"previous \" operation \" failed on this descriptor; close and \"\n                         \"reopen the file to recover\")\n                    {:path (:path handle), :operation operation,\n                     :dao.posix.file/fail-closed true}))))\n\n\n(defn- fail-closed!\n  \"Record operation's failure on the handle (first failure wins).\"\n  [handle operation]\n  (when-let [failed (:failed handle)]\n    (compare-and-set! failed nil operation)))\n\n\n(defn append-frame!\n  \"Write the length-prefixed frame at the current end of the file (fstat):\n   the s32BE length (packed by the wrapper, review F2), then the frame bytes,\n   as one copied buffer. The caller holds the handle lock (dao.jing.file's\n   make-put), which is what makes end-of-file stable here.\n\n   Fail-closed (review F3, J4c): a failure part-way through this write can\n   leave a torn frame at the end of the file, and appending a later valid\n   frame behind it would make the next open refuse or, if the torn bytes\n   happen to parse, truncate acknowledged data. So the failure is recorded on\n   this descriptor (private to the handle, never shared state): every later\n   append or sync through it throws, naming the path and the failed\n   operation; dao.jing.file's get keeps serving its in-memory map (nothing\n   here touches it); close still closes once. Reopening the file runs the\n   shared validated recovery, which drops a torn tail. This is a jolt-only\n   divergence: the JVM, Node and Dart handles stay usable after a failed\n   write (spec §5, Files row). The mark is set by any throwable from the\n   write loop, broader than the design's persistence-uncertain criterion\n   (deepseek's J4c review): a failure that wrote nothing also closes the\n   handle, which is conservative and safe.\"\n  [handle frame]\n  (refuse-if-failed! handle)\n  (let [len (alength frame)\n        total (+ 4 len)\n        end (length handle)]\n    (with-native total\n      (fn [buf]\n        (c-s32be-put buf 0 len)\n        (when (pos? len) (ffi/write-array buf :int8 frame 4))\n        (try (write-from! handle buf end total)\n             (catch Throwable t\n               (fail-closed! handle \"pwrite\")\n               (throw t)))))\n    nil))\n\n\n(defn sync!\n  \"fsync: a full sync of the descriptor, not a port flush and not\n   fdatasync. A put is acknowledged only after it.\n\n   A failed fsync is never retried, here or by the caller, and it makes the\n   handle fail-closed like a failed append (review F3). The reason a retry\n   is unsafe: on Linux a failed fsync may already have dropped the dirty\n   pages it could not write (marking them clean), so a second fsync can\n   answer success while the frame never reached storage. Only a reopen,\n   whose validated recovery reads what the file actually holds, can say\n   which frames stand.\"\n  [handle]\n  (refuse-if-failed! handle)\n  (let [r (fsync-once (fd-of handle))]\n    (when (neg? r)\n      (fail-closed! handle \"fsync\")\n      (throw (io-error handle \"fsync\" (- r) {})))\n    nil))\n\n\n(defn close!\n  \"Release the descriptor: exactly one close attempt per handle, never\n   retried after EINTR (the descriptor is released anyway, and a retry could\n   close one another thread was given since). A second call is a no-op.\"\n  [handle]\n  (when (compare-and-set! (:released handle) false true)\n    (let [r (close-once (:fd handle))]\n      (when (and (neg? r) (not= r (- eintr)))\n        (throw (io-error handle \"close\" (- r) {})))))\n  nil)\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; J4b: directory ownership, bounded reads, HEAD replacement\n;; =============================================================================\n\n;; dao.space.store.fs's :jolt arms call these. Its `held` registry, the\n;; refusal texts and the lock!/atomic-replace! sequences stay there; this is\n;; only the syscalls behind them.\n\n(defn- native-path\n  \"path resolved against user.dir, as java.io.File does (see `open`).\"\n  [path]\n  (.getAbsolutePath (java.io.File. ^String path)))\n\n\n(defn- fs-error\n  [operation path errno data]\n  (ex-info (str \"dao.posix.file \" operation \" failed on \" path \": \"\n                (error-text errno) \" (errno \" errno \")\")\n           (merge {:operation operation, :path path, :errno errno} data)))\n\n\n;; native/jolt-file/jolt_file.c JF_*\n(def ^:private kinds\n  {0 :absent, 1 :regular, 2 :directory, 3 :fifo, 4 :symlink, 5 :other})\n\n\n(defn classify\n  \"What path names, as :absent, :regular, :directory, :fifo, :symlink or\n   :other: lstat without `follow?` (a symlink is :symlink, dangling or not),\n   stat with it (a dangling symlink is :absent). Any failure other than no\n   entry throws; it is never read as absence.\"\n  [path follow?]\n  (let [r (path-bytes! (native-path path) #(c-classify %1 %2 (if follow? 1 0)))]\n    (when (neg? r) (throw (fs-error (if follow? \"stat\" \"lstat\") path (- r) {})))\n    (kinds r)))\n\n\n;; Single system calls the J4b sequences make, through vars for the same\n;; test-only indirection as pread-once: the HEAD seam trace and the injected\n;; lock, rename and directory-sync failures redefine them.\n\n(defn- lock-once\n  [fd]\n  (c-lock fd))\n\n\n(defn- open-excl-once\n  [p n]\n  (c-open-excl p n))\n\n\n(defn- rename-once\n  [from to]\n  (path-bytes! from (fn [a n1] (path-bytes! to (fn [b n2] (c-rename a n1 b n2))))))\n\n\n(defn- sync-dir-once\n  [dir]\n  (path-bytes! dir c-sync-dir))\n\n\n(defn fidentity\n  \"[st_dev st_ino] of the open descriptor fd (the O_CLOEXEC gate compares\n   descriptors by identity, never by number).\"\n  [fd]\n  (with-native 16\n    (fn [out]\n      (let [r (c-fidentity fd out)]\n        (when (neg? r) (throw (fs-error \"fstat\" (str \"fd \" fd) (- r) {})))\n        (vec (ffi/read-array out :uint64 2))))))\n\n\n(defn lock-file!\n  \"Open (creating) the lock file at path, close-on-exec, and take its\n   whole-file fcntl write lock without waiting. Answers the handle on\n   success, :held when another process holds the lock (EAGAIN/EACCES; this\n   handle is closed first, and only this one), and throws on any other\n   failure (closing this handle). The lock is released by close! of the\n   handle, exactly once: a record lock drops with the descriptor.\"\n  [path]\n  (let [fd (path-bytes! (native-path path) c-open)]\n    (when (neg? fd) (throw (fs-error \"open\" path (- fd) {})))\n    (let [h {:fd fd, :path path, :released (atom false)}\n          r (try (lock-once fd)\n                 (catch Throwable t (close! h) (throw t)))]\n      (cond\n        (zero? r) h\n        (= 1 r) (do (close! h) :held)\n        :else (do (close! h) (throw (fs-error \"fcntl(F_SETLK)\" path (- r) {})))))))\n\n\n(defn realpath\n  \"The canonical path of an existing path (realpath).\"\n  [path]\n  (with-native 4096\n    (fn [buf]\n      (let [r (path-bytes! (native-path path) #(c-realpath %1 %2 buf 4096))]\n        (when (neg? r) (throw (fs-error \"realpath\" path (- r) {})))\n        (text/utf8-decode (ffi/read-array buf r))))))\n\n\n(defn- read-to!\n  \"pread from fd into buf[0, cap) until end of file or cap bytes: the count.\n   Short reads continue, EINTR retries, an error throws.\"\n  [path fd buf cap]\n  (loop [done 0]\n    (if (>= done cap)\n      done\n      (let [r (pread-once fd done buf done (- cap done))]\n        (cond\n          (pos? r) (recur (+ done r))\n          (zero? r) done\n          (= r (- eintr)) (recur done)\n          :else (throw (fs-error \"pread\" path (- r) {:read done})))))))\n\n\n(defn- with-read-descriptor\n  \"(f fd) over path opened read-only, non-blocking and close-on-exec, the\n   descriptor rechecked by fstat: :not-regular unless it is a regular file.\n   Closed however f exits.\"\n  [path f]\n  (let [fd (path-bytes! (native-path path) c-open-read)]\n    (when (neg? fd) (throw (fs-error \"open\" path (- fd) {})))\n    (let [h {:fd fd, :path path, :released (atom false)}]\n      (try\n        (let [k (c-fclassify fd)]\n          (when (neg? k) (throw (fs-error \"fstat\" path (- k) {})))\n          (if (= :regular (kinds k)) (f fd) :not-regular))\n        (finally (close! h))))))\n\n\n(def ^:private max-bound\n  ;; the JVM arm reads (int (inc limit)) bytes: one more than the limit must\n  ;; fit a signed 32-bit count\n  2147483646)\n\n\n(defn read-bounded\n  \"At most limit + 1 bytes of the regular file at path, as a fresh byte\n   array; :absent when no entry exists there (lstat); :not-regular for a\n   dangling symlink, a directory, a FIFO or anything else that is not a\n   regular file, decided by stat before the open and by fstat of the\n   non-blocking descriptor after it, so a FIFO never blocks. A symlink to a\n   regular file is followed.\"\n  [path limit]\n  (when-not (and (integer? limit) (<= 0 limit max-bound))\n    (throw (ex-info (str \"read-bounded limit out of range: \" limit)\n                    {:path path, :limit limit})))\n  (cond\n    (= :absent (classify path false)) :absent\n    (not= :regular (classify path true)) :not-regular\n    :else (let [cap (inc limit)]\n            (with-native cap\n              (fn [buf]\n                (with-read-descriptor path\n                  (fn [fd]\n                    (let [n (read-to! path fd buf cap)]\n                      (if (zero? n) (byte-array 0) (ffi/read-array buf n))))))))))\n\n\n(def ^:private text-chunk (* 64 1024))\n\n\n(defn read-all\n  \"Every byte of the regular file at path (following a symlink), or :absent\n   when nothing is there (stat); :not-regular otherwise. Read to end of file\n   in bounded chunks.\"\n  [path]\n  (case (classify path true)\n    :absent :absent\n    :regular (with-native text-chunk\n               (fn [buf]\n                 (with-read-descriptor path\n                   (fn [fd]\n                     (loop [offset 0, acc (transient [])]\n                       (let [n (loop [done 0]\n                                 (if (>= done text-chunk)\n                                   done\n                                   (let [r (pread-once fd (+ offset done) buf done\n                                                       (- text-chunk done))]\n                                     (cond\n                                       (pos? r) (recur (+ done r))\n                                       (zero? r) done\n                                       (= r (- eintr)) (recur done)\n                                       :else (throw (fs-error \"pread\" path (- r)\n                                                              {:offset offset}))))))\n                             acc (if (pos? n)\n                                   (reduce conj! acc (ffi/read-array buf n))\n                                   acc)]\n                         (if (< n text-chunk)\n                           (byte-array (persistent! acc))\n                           (recur (+ offset n) acc))))))))\n    :not-regular))\n\n\n(defn- write-new-from!\n  \"Write every byte of bs to the descriptor fd just created for path, fsync,\n   then close with its outcome checked (closing on any failure first).\"\n  [path fd bs]\n  (let [h {:fd fd, :path path, :released (atom false)}\n        n (alength bs)]\n    (try\n      (when (pos? n)\n        (with-native n\n          (fn [buf]\n            (ffi/write-array buf bs)\n            (write-from! h buf 0 n))))\n      (sync! h)\n      (catch Throwable t\n        (try (close! h) (catch Throwable _ nil))\n        (throw t)))\n    (close! h)))\n\n\n(defn write-new-synced!\n  \"The HEAD temp, steps 1-3 of the replacement: create path exclusively\n   (O_CREAT | O_EXCL, mode 0666 under the umask, close-on-exec), write every\n   byte of bs (short writes continue, EINTR retries, zero progress throws),\n   fsync, then close with its outcome checked. The temp is left in place on\n   failure, as the other hosts leave theirs; nothing here renames.\"\n  [path bs]\n  (let [fd (path-bytes! (native-path path) open-excl-once)]\n    (when (neg? fd) (throw (fs-error \"open(O_EXCL)\" path (- fd) {})))\n    (write-new-from! path fd bs)))\n\n\n(def ^:private eexist (c-eexist))\n\n\n(defn write-new-private!\n  \"A key file (J4d): create path exclusively with mode 0600 (owner-only;\n   the umask can only narrow it), close-on-exec, write every byte of bs,\n   fsync and close checked. Answers :exists, writing nothing, when any entry\n   is already there: a key file is never overwritten.\"\n  [path bs]\n  (let [fd (path-bytes! (native-path path) c-open-private)]\n    (cond\n      (= fd (- eexist)) :exists\n      (neg? fd) (throw (fs-error \"open(O_EXCL, 0600)\" path (- fd) {}))\n      :else (do (write-new-from! path fd bs) nil))))\n\n\n(defn rename!\n  \"rename(from, to), atomic within one directory: no copy-and-delete\n   fallback, so a cross-device rename fails.\"\n  [from to]\n  (let [r (rename-once (native-path from) (native-path to))]\n    (when (neg? r) (throw (fs-error \"rename\" from (- r) {:to to})))\n    nil))\n\n\n(defn sync-dir!\n  \"Open the directory, fsync it, close it: a rename in it is then durable,\n   not merely visible. A failure throws; it is never read as a host without\n   directory sync.\"\n  [dir]\n  (let [r (sync-dir-once (native-path dir))]\n    (when (neg? r) (throw (fs-error \"fsync(directory)\" dir (- r) {})))\n    nil))\n(ns dao.space.store.fs\n  \"Explicit host file operations for the durable index store directory\n   (dao.space.store; docs/design/yin.repl.dao.space-index.md, the\n   durable-store design's host section): an exclusive directory lock held by the one process\n   that owns the store, and an atomic write-temp, sync, rename\n   replacement for HEAD. CLJ, Node, and Dart each implement them with\n   their own primitives; nothing here falls back silently.\n\n   Lock ownership is an operating-system fact on the JVM — an exclusive\n   `FileChannel` lock on `<dir>/lock`, released when the holding process\n   dies — and on Dart, `RandomAccessFile.lockSync`. Node has no flock in\n   its core, so each contender creates its own uniquely named claim\n   entry, `lock.<pid>.<nonce>`, and then reads the others: a live one\n   refuses, and a dead one — an owner or a contender that crashed at any\n   point — is removed without a race, because a unique name is never\n   created again.  A crashed process never bricks the directory, and two\n   contenders never both own it (`node-lock!`) — two worker threads of\n   one process included, since a claim naming this pid is live unless it\n   is this call's own entry.\n\n   Contract, pid reuse (Node only): a claim's liveness is its pid's, so a\n   crashed owner's claim whose pid the OS has since given to an\n   unrelated live process reads as live, and startup refuses — the safe\n   direction; it is never read as dead while alive.  The refusal names\n   the claim entries it saw.  Operator remedy: confirm that no REPL uses\n   the directory, then delete the named `<dir>/lock.<pid>.<nonce>`\n   entries (or wait until the process holding that pid exits); the next\n   start opens normally.  The JVM and Dart locks are held by the\n   operating system and have no pid-reuse case.\n\n   HEAD replacement writes the whole record to a temp file in the same\n   directory — every byte of it, however many writes the host takes —\n   syncs it, and renames it over HEAD — rename is the atomicity, so a\n   torn, short, or interrupted write is never observed as HEAD.\n   The directory itself is then synced where the host can\n   (`directory-sync-supported?`: Node and the JVM on POSIX systems), and\n   there a failed sync fails the replacement; a host that cannot sync a\n   directory at all (Dart's core, Windows) is not a failed sync.\n\n   The operating-system locks are per process — a POSIX `fcntl` lock\n   never refuses its own process — so this process's own holdings are\n   kept here too, in `held`: a second owner inside the one process is\n   refused by that record before any second handle on the lock file is\n   opened.  `held` is process-global mutable state, an explicit\n   exception to the no-hidden-global-state invariant: it mirrors a fact\n   the host itself keeps per process (which files this process has\n   locked), so it can be no narrower than the process, and it holds\n   nothing but the canonical paths of the directories this process\n   currently owns.  On Node each worker thread has its own `held`; across\n   workers the claim entries decide.\"\n  (:require #?@(:cljd [[\"dart:convert\" :as convert]\n                       [\"dart:io\" :as dart-io]]\n                :jolt [[dao.host.text :as text]\n                       [dao.posix.file :as posix-file]])\n            [clojure.string :as str])\n  #?@(:cljd [(:import [\"dart:typed_data\" Uint8List])]))\n\n\n(def lock-name\n  \"The lock file's own name inside the durable store directory, and the\n   prefix of Node's claim entries (`lock.<pid>.<nonce>`).  Its presence\n   is bookkeeping, never authority: the JVM and Dart locks live on open\n   handles, and each Node entry names a pid that is checked for life — a\n   reused pid keeps a dead claim live until an operator removes the\n   entry (see the namespace docstring's pid-reuse contract).\"\n  \"lock\")\n\n\n(defn- refuse-locked\n  [dir]\n  (throw (ex-info (str dir \" is locked by another process\")\n                  {:dir dir})))\n\n\n(defn- read-text!\n  \"The file at `path` as text — trimmed, for one-record files read whole —\n   or nil when absent.\"\n  [path]\n  #?(:cljd (let [f (dart-io/File. path)]\n             (when (.existsSync f)\n               (str/trim (.readAsStringSync f))))\n     ;; a descriptor read, strictly decoded: absence is nil; a permission or\n     ;; I/O error, or an entry that is not a regular file, throws\n     :jolt (let [bs (posix-file/read-all path)]\n             (case bs\n               :absent nil\n               :not-regular (throw (ex-info (str path \" is not a regular file\")\n                                            {:path path}))\n               (str/trim (text/utf8-decode bs))))\n     :clj (let [f (java.io.File. ^String path)]\n            (when (.exists f) (str/trim (slurp f))))\n     :cljs (let [fs (js/require \"fs\")]\n             (when (.existsSync ^js fs path)\n               (str/trim (.readFileSync ^js fs path \"utf8\"))))))\n\n\n#?(:cljs\n   (defn- live-pid?\n     \"True when a pid names a live process — the lock file's owner is\n      alive.  A process this one may not signal (`EPERM`) is alive too.\"\n     [pid]\n     (try (.kill js/process pid 0)\n          true\n          (catch :default e\n            (= \"EPERM\" (.-code e))))))\n\n\n#?(:cljs\n   (defn- claim-entries\n     \"Every Node claim entry in the directory, as `[file-name pid]`: the\n      files `lock.<pid>.<nonce>`, each created by the one process whose\n      pid it names.\"\n     [fs dir]\n     (keep (fn [file-name]\n             (when-some [[_ pid] (re-matches #\"lock\\.(\\d+)\\.[0-9a-f-]+\"\n                                             file-name)]\n               [file-name (js/parseInt pid 10)]))\n           (.readdirSync ^js fs dir))))\n\n\n#?(:cljs\n   (defn- node-lock!\n     \"Claim the directory on Node, answering the release, or refuse.\n\n      Each contender creates its own uniquely named claim entry,\n      `lock.<pid>.<nonce>`, and only then reads the others.  An entry\n      naming a live process is an owner or a rival contender: the claim\n      is withdrawn and refused.  An entry naming a dead process — an\n      owner that crashed, or a contender that crashed midway through its\n      own claim — is removed, and that removal is race-free because the\n      name is unique: no later process ever creates it again, so it can\n      never be a newer live claim.  Of two contenders, each creates\n      before it reads, so at most one reads no live rival; at worst two\n      simultaneous starts both refuse, never both own.\n\n      An entry naming this very pid is live like any other: worker\n      threads share the process's pid but each has its own `held`, so\n      another worker's claim looks exactly like that — only this call's\n      own entry, by its exact name, is this contender's.  Liveness is by\n      pid, so a dead claim whose pid was reused by an unrelated live\n      process is read as live and refuses; the refusal names the claim\n      entries it saw, for the operator remedy in the namespace\n      docstring.\"\n     [dir claimed]\n     (let [fs (js/require \"fs\")\n           own-pid (.-pid js/process)\n           own (str lock-name \".\" own-pid \".\" (random-uuid))\n           own-path (str dir \"/\" own)\n           release! (fn []\n                      (try (.unlinkSync ^js fs own-path)\n                           (catch :default _ nil)))]\n       (.closeSync ^js fs (.openSync ^js fs own-path \"wx\"))\n       (try\n         (when claimed (claimed))\n         (let [live (into []\n                          (remove\n                            (fn [[file-name pid]]\n                              (when-not (live-pid? pid)\n                                (try (.unlinkSync ^js fs (str dir \"/\" file-name))\n                                     (catch :default _ nil))\n                                true)))\n                          (remove #(= own (first %)) (claim-entries fs dir)))]\n           (when (seq live)\n             (throw (ex-info (str dir \" is locked by another process\"\n                                  \" (claims: \"\n                                  (str/join \", \" (map first live)) \")\")\n                             {:dir dir :claims (mapv first live)})))\n           release!)\n         (catch :default e\n           (release!)\n           (throw e))))))\n\n\n(defonce ^:private held\n  ;; The host-ownership exception to the no-hidden-global-state invariant\n  ;; (see the namespace docstring): the canonical paths of the durable\n  ;; directories this process holds locked.\n  (atom #{}))\n\n\n(defn- canonical-dir\n  \"The directory's one canonical path: the key of this process's own\n   holdings, so two spellings of one directory are one owner.\"\n  [dir]\n  #?(:cljd (.resolveSymbolicLinksSync (dart-io/Directory. dir))\n     :jolt (posix-file/realpath dir)\n     :clj (.getCanonicalPath (java.io.File. ^String dir))\n     :cljs (.realpathSync ^js (js/require \"fs\") dir)))\n\n\n(defn- claim!\n  \"Record `key` as held by this process, answering false when it already\n   was — a second owner inside the one process, refused before it opens\n   any handle on the lock file (closing such a handle would drop this\n   process's `fcntl` lock).\"\n  [key]\n  (let [claimed? (volatile! false)]\n    (swap! held (fn [keys]\n                  (vreset! claimed? (not (contains? keys key)))\n                  (conj keys key)))\n    @claimed?))\n\n\n(defn- host-lock!\n  \"Take the host's exclusive lock on `path`, answering its release\n   function, or refuse naming the directory.\"\n  #_{:clj-kondo/ignore [:unused-binding]}\n  [dir path claimed]\n  #?(:cljd (let [raf (.openSync (dart-io/File. path)\n                                .mode dart-io/FileMode.append)]\n             (try\n               (.lockSync raf)\n               (fn []\n                 (try (.unlockSync raf) (catch Object _ nil))\n                 (try (.closeSync raf) (catch Object _ nil)))\n               (catch Object _\n                 (try (.closeSync raf) (catch Object _ nil))\n                 (refuse-locked dir))))\n     ;; the JVM's FileChannel.tryLock in the same family: a whole-file\n     ;; fcntl(F_SETLK) write lock, close-on-exec; held elsewhere refuses after\n     ;; closing this handle only, and the release is that one close\n     :jolt (let [h (posix-file/lock-file! path)]\n             (if (= :held h)\n               (refuse-locked dir)\n               (fn []\n                 (try (posix-file/close! h) (catch Throwable _ nil)))))\n     :clj (let [f (java.io.RandomAccessFile. ^String path \"rw\")]\n            (try\n              (let [fl (try (.tryLock (.getChannel f))\n                            (catch java.nio.channels.OverlappingFileLockException _\n                              nil))]\n                (if (nil? fl)\n                  (do (.close f)\n                      (refuse-locked dir))\n                  (fn []\n                    (try (.release ^java.nio.channels.FileLock fl)\n                         (catch Exception _ nil))\n                    (try (.close f)\n                         (catch Exception _ nil)))))\n              (catch Throwable t\n                (try (.close f) (catch Exception _ nil))\n                (throw t))))\n     :cljs (node-lock! dir claimed)))\n\n\n(defn lock!\n  \"Acquire the exclusive lock of the durable store directory, or refuse\n   naming the directory — whether the other owner is another process or\n   this one. The answer is a plain release; closing whatever the host\n   needs closed is all releasing takes.\n\n   `claimed`, `(fn [])`, runs on Node once this process's claim entry\n   exists and before it reads the others — the window a concurrent\n   contender can race — so the tests can interleave one there.\"\n  ([dir] (lock! dir {}))\n  ([dir {:keys [claimed]}]\n   (let [key (canonical-dir dir)]\n     (when-not (claim! key)\n       (refuse-locked dir))\n     (let [release! (try (host-lock! dir (str dir \"/\" lock-name) claimed)\n                         (catch #?(:cljd Object\n                                   :clj Throwable\n                                   :cljs :default)\n                                e\n                           (swap! held disj key)\n                           (throw e)))\n           released? (volatile! false)]\n       {:release! (fn []\n                    (when-not @released?\n                      (vreset! released? true)\n                      (release!)\n                      (swap! held disj key)))}))))\n\n\n(defn unlock!\n  \"Release a lock `lock!` answered. Idempotent: a released lock releases\n   again as a no-op, never a later owner's lock of the same directory.\"\n  [lock]\n  (when-let [release! (:release! lock)]\n    (release!)))\n\n\n(defn directory-sync-supported?\n  \"True when this host can open a directory and fsync it, so a HEAD\n   replacement's rename can be made durable, not merely visible: Node\n   and the JVM on POSIX systems can; Windows cannot open a directory for\n   sync, and Dart's core has no directory sync at all.\"\n  []\n  #?(:cljd false\n     :jolt true ; macOS and Linux only (the qualified jolt platforms)\n     :clj (not (str/starts-with? (str (System/getProperty \"os.name\"))\n                                 \"Windows\"))\n     :cljs (not= \"win32\" (.-platform js/process))))\n\n\n(defn- sync-failure\n  [dir e]\n  (ex-info (str \"cannot sync the directory \" dir \" after the rename: \" e)\n           {:dir dir}\n           e))\n\n\n#?(:cljs\n   (defn- sync-directory!\n     \"Sync the directory entry itself where the host supports it — the\n      rename is durable, not merely visible.  There, a failure throws: a\n      replacement whose directory entry is not known durable is not\n      reported done.\"\n     [fs dir]\n     (when (directory-sync-supported?)\n       (try (let [fd (.openSync ^js fs dir \"r\")]\n              (try (.fsyncSync ^js fs fd)\n                   (finally\n                     (.closeSync ^js fs fd))))\n            (catch :default e\n              (throw (sync-failure dir e)))))))\n\n\n#?(:cljd nil\n   :jolt\n   (defn- sync-directory!\n     \"Sync the directory entry itself (open, fsync, close): the rename is\n      durable, not merely visible.  A failure throws; it never turns the\n      host into one without directory sync.\"\n     [dir]\n     (when (directory-sync-supported?)\n       (try (posix-file/sync-dir! dir)\n            (catch Throwable e\n              (throw (sync-failure dir e))))))\n   :clj\n   (defn- sync-directory!\n     \"Sync the directory entry itself where the host supports it — the\n      rename is durable, not merely visible.  There, a failure throws: a\n      replacement whose directory entry is not known durable is not\n      reported done.\"\n     [dir]\n     (when (directory-sync-supported?)\n       (try (with-open [ch (java.nio.channels.FileChannel/open\n                             (java.nio.file.Paths/get ^String dir\n                                                      (make-array String 0))\n                             ^\"[Ljava.nio.file.OpenOption;\"\n                             (into-array java.nio.file.OpenOption\n                                         [java.nio.file.StandardOpenOption/READ]))]\n              (.force ch true))\n            (catch Exception e\n              (throw (sync-failure dir e)))))))\n\n\n#?(:cljs\n   (defn- write-fully!\n     \"Write every byte of `buf` to `fd`.  `writeSync` may store fewer\n      bytes than asked and says how many in its answer, so the rest is\n      written from there until none remain; a write that stores nothing\n      fails rather than looping.  (The JVM's `OutputStream.write(byte[])`\n      and Dart's `writeAsStringSync` write the whole buffer or throw.)\"\n     [fs fd buf]\n     (loop [offset 0]\n       (when (< offset (.-length buf))\n         (let [written (.writeSync ^js fs fd buf offset\n                                   (- (.-length buf) offset))]\n           (when-not (pos? written)\n             (throw (ex-info \"the write stored no bytes\"\n                             {:offset offset :length (.-length buf)})))\n           (recur (+ offset written)))))))\n\n\n(defn atomic-replace!\n  \"Replace `<dir>/<name>` with `text` atomically: write the whole record\n   to a temp file beside it, sync the temp, rename it over the target,\n   and sync the directory where the host supports it — a failed\n   directory sync fails the replacement. A reader observes either the\n   whole previous record or the whole new one, never a partial write.\n\n   `before-rename`, `(fn [temp-path])`, runs in the one window a crash\n   can interrupt — after the temp is synced, before the rename — so a\n   test can interrupt the replacement there; a throw leaves the target\n   exactly as it was.\"\n  ([dir name text] (atomic-replace! dir name text {}))\n  ([dir name text {:keys [before-rename]}]\n   (let [target (str dir \"/\" name)\n         temp (str dir \"/\" name \".tmp-\" (random-uuid))]\n     #?(:cljd (try (.writeAsStringSync (dart-io/File. temp) text .flush true)\n                   (when before-rename (before-rename temp))\n                   (.renameSync (dart-io/File. temp) target)\n                   (catch Object e\n                     (throw (ex-info (str \"cannot replace \" target \": \" e)\n                                     {:dir dir :target target}\n                                     e))))\n        ;; temp (O_CREAT | O_EXCL, 0666 under the umask) -> write-all ->\n        ;; fsync -> close -> before-rename -> rename -> directory sync\n        :jolt (try (posix-file/write-new-synced! temp (text/utf8-encode text))\n                   (when before-rename (before-rename temp))\n                   (posix-file/rename! temp target)\n                   (sync-directory! dir)\n                   (catch Throwable e\n                     (throw (ex-info (str \"cannot replace \" target \": \" e)\n                                     {:dir dir :target target}\n                                     e))))\n        :clj (try (with-open [out (java.io.FileOutputStream. ^String temp)]\n                    (.write out (.getBytes ^String text \"UTF-8\"))\n                    (.flush out)\n                    (.sync (.getFD out)))\n                  (when before-rename (before-rename temp))\n                  (java.nio.file.Files/move\n                    (java.nio.file.Paths/get temp (make-array String 0))\n                    (java.nio.file.Paths/get target (make-array String 0))\n                    (into-array java.nio.file.StandardCopyOption\n                                [java.nio.file.StandardCopyOption/ATOMIC_MOVE\n                                 java.nio.file.StandardCopyOption/REPLACE_EXISTING]))\n                  (sync-directory! dir)\n                  (catch Throwable e\n                    (throw (ex-info (str \"cannot replace \" target \": \" e)\n                                    {:dir dir :target target}\n                                    e))))\n        :cljs (let [fs (js/require \"fs\")]\n                (try (let [fd (.openSync ^js fs temp \"w\")]\n                       (try (write-fully! fs fd (js/Buffer.from text \"utf8\"))\n                            (.fsyncSync ^js fs fd)\n                            (finally\n                              (.closeSync ^js fs fd))))\n                     (when before-rename (before-rename temp))\n                     (.renameSync ^js fs temp target)\n                     (sync-directory! fs dir)\n                     (catch :default e\n                       (throw (ex-info (str \"cannot replace \" target \": \" e)\n                                       {:dir dir :target target}\n                                       e)))))))))\n\n\n(defn read-file-text\n  \"The file `<dir>/<name>` as text, or nil when absent — the public read\n   the store's HEAD parsing uses.\"\n  [dir name]\n  (read-text! (str dir \"/\" name)))\n\n\n(defn- not-regular\n  []\n  (ex-info \"it is not a regular file\" {}))\n\n\n(defn read-bounded\n  \"At most `limit` bytes of the file at `path`, one more when it holds\n   more, or nil when no entry exists there.  Never reads the whole of a\n   larger file into memory, and never opens what is not a regular file\n   (a directory, a FIFO, a device), which would block or fail: that is\n   refused first.  A symlink is followed when its target is a regular\n   file; a dangling one is refused.  The bytes are the host's own: a\n   `Uint8List` on Dart, a `byte[]` on the JVM, a `Buffer` on Node\n   (`byte-length` counts them, `decode-utf8` reads them).\"\n  [path limit]\n  ;; On Dart and the JVM the type is checked, then the file is opened:\n  ;; a regular file swapped for a FIFO between the two would still block\n  ;; the open.  Accepted (Architect, H3 round 3): the swap needs write\n  ;; access to this node's own locked store directory, which can already\n  ;; replace HEAD, and the worst outcome is a startup that blocks, never\n  ;; a wrong head.  Node and jolt open non-blocking and check the opened\n  ;; descriptor, so they are not exposed.\n  #?(:cljd (let [t (.-type (.statSync (dart-io/File. path)))]\n             (cond\n               (= t dart-io/FileSystemEntityType.notFound)\n               ;; a dangling link is an entry, not absence\n               (when (.existsSync (dart-io/Link. path))\n                 (throw (not-regular)))\n\n               (not= t dart-io/FileSystemEntityType.file)\n               (throw (not-regular))\n\n               :else\n               (let [raf (.openSync (dart-io/File. path))]\n                 (try (.readSync raf (inc limit))\n                      (finally (.closeSync raf))))))\n     :jolt (let [bs (posix-file/read-bounded path limit)]\n             ;; classify (lstat, stat), then a non-blocking open rechecked\n             ;; by fstat: a FIFO swapped in never blocks\n             (case bs\n               :absent nil\n               :not-regular (throw (not-regular))\n               bs))\n     :clj (let [p (java.nio.file.Paths/get path (make-array String 0))]\n            (cond\n              (java.nio.file.Files/notExists\n                p (into-array java.nio.file.LinkOption\n                              [java.nio.file.LinkOption/NOFOLLOW_LINKS]))\n              nil\n\n              (not (java.nio.file.Files/isRegularFile\n                     p (make-array java.nio.file.LinkOption 0)))\n              (throw (not-regular))\n\n              :else\n              (with-open [in (java.nio.file.Files/newInputStream\n                               p (make-array java.nio.file.OpenOption 0))]\n                (.readNBytes in (int (inc limit))))))\n     :cljs (let [fs (js/require \"fs\")\n                 entry? (try (.lstatSync fs path) true\n                             (catch :default _ false))]\n             (when entry?\n               (when-not (try (.isFile (.statSync fs path))\n                              (catch :default _ false))\n                 (throw (not-regular)))\n               ;; non-blocking, then the opened resource is checked again\n               (let [fd (.openSync fs path (bit-or (.. fs -constants -O_RDONLY)\n                                                   (.. fs -constants -O_NONBLOCK)))\n                     buf (js/Buffer.alloc (inc limit))]\n                 (try\n                   (when-not (.isFile (.fstatSync fs fd))\n                     (throw (not-regular)))\n                   (loop [off 0]\n                     (let [n (.readSync fs fd buf off (- (inc limit) off) nil)\n                           off' (+ off n)]\n                       (if (and (pos? n) (< off' (inc limit)))\n                         (recur off')\n                         (.subarray buf 0 off'))))\n                   (finally (.closeSync fs fd))))))))\n\n\n(defn byte-length\n  \"The number of bytes `bs`, as `read-bounded` answers them, holds.\"\n  [bs]\n  #?(:cljd (.-length ^Uint8List bs)\n     :clj (alength ^bytes bs)\n     :cljs (.-length bs)))\n\n\n(defn decode-utf8\n  \"`bs`, as `read-bounded` answers them, as text, strictly: invalid UTF-8\n   throws, never decodes to a replacement character.  A leading byte\n   order mark is the host's to keep or drop: a caller that ignores one\n   strips it from the text.\"\n  [bs]\n  ;; Dart's `utf8` codec does not allow malformed input: it throws a\n  ;; FormatException\n  #?(:cljd (.decode convert/utf8 ^Uint8List bs)\n     :jolt (text/utf8-decode bs)\n     :clj (str (.decode (doto (.newDecoder java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets/UTF_8)\n                          (.onMalformedInput\n                            java.nio.charset.CodingErrorAction/REPORT)\n                          (.onUnmappableCharacter\n                            java.nio.charset.CodingErrorAction/REPORT))\n                        (java.nio.ByteBuffer/wrap ^bytes bs)))\n     :cljs (.decode (js/TextDecoder. \"utf-8\" #js {:fatal true}) bs)))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
