ading order\"> &lt; </a>]</td>\n17627:<td valign=\"middle\" align=\"left\">[<a href=\"#Byte_002dport-settings\" title=\"Next section in reading order\"> &gt; </a>]</td>\n17645:<tr><td align=\"left\" valign=\"top\"><a href=\"#Byte_002dport-settings\">14.6.1 Byte-port settings</a></td><td>&nbsp;&nbsp;</td><td align=\"left\" valign=\"top\"></td></tr>\n17650:<a name=\"Byte_002dport-settings\"></a>\n17667:<a name=\"Byte_002dport-settings-1\"></a>\n17869:<tr><td valign=\"middle\" align=\"left\">[<a href=\"#Byte_002dport-settings\" title=\"Previous section in reading order\"> &lt; </a>]</td>\n15:   recovery data, an immutable publication policy, and correlated generic\n25:   The indexer publishes through the node's store: each put inserts\n26:   locally and appends one replicate request, and the manifest reads back\n28:   announces the publication to the node.  Section 3's ordering contract\n38:   socket bound, and for each publication its result (acknowledged,\n57:   The published head trace (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md,\n60:   * A publisher (a key, a socket and `:publish?`) deposits its HEAD's\n108:   publishing node accepts from peers' `:store` requests before it\n117:   their own while a repair is queued.  This store publishes 64-key index\n119:   generic 512-key page did, and every live publication repairs its own:\n145:(defn- refused-head\n172:(defn parse-arg\n187:(defn- dht-refusal\n192:  [{:keys [dir peers publish? bind-host bind-port max-inbound-bytes manifest\n205:    (not (boolean? publish?))\n206:    \"the DHT publication declaration must be true or false\"\n229:  \"The DHT store spec's defaults: solo (no peers), publishing off, bound\n233:   :publish? false\n240:(defn checked-spec\n268:(defn- read-edn\n275:(defn- read-one\n292:(defn parse-heads\n314:(defn render-heads\n321:(defn- without-boms\n335:(defn read-heads\n375:(defn- ring\n382:(defn- deposit-head!\n396:(defn- publisher\n397:  \"The board composition of a publishing node: a key, a socket (peers)\n398:   and `:publish?` (5.4), or nil.\"\n399:  [{:keys [peers publish?]} key base]\n400:  (when (and (map? key) (:seed key) (:public key) publish? (seq peers))\n404:       :principal (sign/principal (:public key))\n415:(defn followed-principals\n425:   policy — the byte ceiling is what bounds a publication now, and a count\n431:(defn publication-policy\n432:  \"The immutable generic publication policy this composition declares over\n442:   A publication too large for that ceiling is cut into smaller pages\n451:(defn link-source\n462:   `opts` carries the session's own link inputs — `:key`, the publisher's\n463:   key; `:principals`, the declared publishers; `:admission`, the admission\n475:(defn- checked-follow\n502:(defn- heads-records\n517:(defn- follower-of\n540:(defn- compose\n543:   transport step-owner value this composition keeps.  The publisher's\n547:   publication policy cross inward\n551:  (let [pub (publisher checked key base)\n554:               pub (assoc ::publisher pub)\n566:               (and manifest (not= manifest (get-in base [:recovery\n572:    (when-some [recovered (and pub (get-in base [:recovery :manifest]))]\n575:            ;; The generic publication policy this composition declares\n579:            ;; are the transport's own answer (`publication-policy`): the\n585:            :publication-policy (publication-policy node)\n586:            :head-fn (fn [manifest-address]\n587:                       ((:head-fn base) manifest-address)\n590:            :recovery (:recovery base)\n598:(defn- quietly!\n606:(defn open\n615:                  with, carrying :head-fn, :recovery, :publication-policy\n618:    :recovery <what the open validated, {:manifest :datoms}>\n619:    :publication-policy <the immutable generic policy of section 5>\n623:   `opts`: `:key`, the publisher key `{:seed :public}`, which makes a\n624:   publishing node deposit its head; `:ws`, the host WebSocket seam\n637:   (let [{:keys [dir peers publish? bind-host bind-port max-inbound-bytes\n645:                                         (get-in base [:recovery :manifest]))]\n650:                         :publish? publish?\n676:          :recovery (:recovery index-store)\n677:          :publication-policy (:publication-policy index-store)\n684:(defn follower\n691:(defn stop!\n712:                   (get-in node [::publisher :server])\n713:                   (update-in [::publisher :server] head.board/stop!)\n719:(defn stopped?\n723:  (let [server (get-in session [:node ::publisher :server])]\n727:(defn close!\n743:        server (get-in node [::publisher :server])]\n753:(defn- address-text\n758:(defn- reason-text\n764:    :dao.jing.dht/unpublished (str \"publication is off: this node fetches \"\n765:                                   \"only; start with --dht-publish to share\")\n772:    :dao.space.dht/backlog-full \"the replicate backlog was full\"\n773:    :dao.space.dht/publications-full (str \"too many publications awaited \"\n782:   :dao.space.dht/displaced \"displaced by newer publications\"})\n785:(defn- result-text\n786:  \"A publication's result (yin.vm.linker.dht.md 5.5.6): when it is not\n790:   publication (docs/design/yin.repl.dht-composition.md section 3).\"\n806:(defn- failure-text\n819:(defn- event-line\n826:                (if (:publish? node) \"; publishing\" \"; fetch-only\"))\n     :ledger-cursor (oldest ledger)\n     :origin nil\n     :reading nil\n     :window (new-ledger)\n     :window-lost? false\n     :pubs []\n     :next-id 0\n     :fresh []\n     :repair []\n     :queued {}\n     :repair-owner {}\n     :inflight {}\n     :misses {}\n     :client (content.step/client-state requests answers (oldest answers))\n     :loads {}\n     :refusal nil\n     :handle {:put-bytes-fn (fn [address bs]\n                              (let [verdict ((:put-bytes-fn front) address bs)]\n                                (stream/append! ledger {::put address})\n                                verdict))\n              :get-bytes-fn (:get-bytes-fn local)\n              :close-fn (fn []\n                          (when-not @closed?\n                            (vreset! closed? true)\n                            (try (when seam ((:close! seam)))\n                                 (finally\n                                   (jing/close! local)))))}}))\n\n\n(defn join\nbb yin-repl --backend jvm         # default; the canonical JVM REPL\nbb yin-repl --backend node        # builds target/yin-repl.js on first use\nbb yin-repl --backend dart        # builds build/yin-repl-peer on first use\nbb yin-repl --backend jolt-chez   # bootstraps jolt if needed; identity-gated\nbb yin-repl --backend jolt-gambit # compiled Yin boot cached in build/gambit-yin/\n```\n\n`jolt-gambit` runs a REAL Yin session under the Gambit backend (the compiled\nboot; interactive terminal passthrough verified on a genuine PTY), with honest\ncapability degradation per the C4 ruling: no `:yang/python` frontend\n(`unavailable-parser` refusal), blocking FFI refuses inline with the exact\ndocumented message, and the session survives both. The qualified FFI tier\n(loader/memory/ordinary calls/errno — two-platform: macOS arm64 and Linux\nx86_64) is callable in-session. Evidence: `make gambityinrepl` in the jolt\nclone (both-platform PASS transcripts); the acceptance gate is\n`host/gambit/yin-repl-session.sh` — a 12-step ordered state machine over real\n`yin>` replies. Yin args pass through (`--index-store mem`, etc.). Per-backend\ncapability summary: JVM/Node/Dart = full (all frontends, all stores);\n`jolt-chez` = full minus native-parser availability as provisioned;\n`jolt-gambit` = core Yin (Clojure frontend, yin.vm, qualified FFI) with\nsubprocess/blocking-capability frontends refusing inline.\n\n### Local In-Memory REPL\nStart an interactive prompt on the JVM with an in-memory index store:\n\n```bash\nclj -M:clj-yin-repl\n```\n\n> **Note for fresh clones:** `yin.repl` loads the generated ANTLR Python3 parser on startup. Run `bb gen:antlr` (or `clj -M:antlr-gen` for JVM) once before starting the REPL or running tests.\n\n### Durable Local Store\nTo persist definitions and evaluated code across restarts, pass a `file:` directory store:\n- **Local-only command & non-interaction with `state.edn`:**\n  - `(use-store ...)` always executes on the local driver (even while attached to a remote server via `(connect ...)`) and is refused over remote `--port` evaluation requests (`Error: (use-store ...) is a local host command and cannot be run over a remote endpoint`).\n  - Like `(vm :type)` and `(trust ...)` / `(untrust ...)`, `(use-store ...)` never reads or writes `<node-dir>/state.edn`, so saved startup profiles are never clobbered by exploratory in-session store switches.\n\n- **Gambit boundary (`jolt-gambit`, owner ruling C4):**\n  - On `jolt-gambit`, `(use-store \"mem\")` (or `:mem`) on a quiesced session opens a fresh in-memory store and preserves the Gambit host modules (`jolt.ffi` and `dao.jing.mem`; re-require them in the rebuilt session before calling their exports).\n  - Durable targets `(use-store \"file:<dir>\")` and `(use-store \"dht:<dir>\" ...)` hit the C4 blocking-FFI refusal inline during `open-first`:\n    `Error: blocking ffi is unsupported on the gambit target — GC-safe blocking is unqualified on Gambit 4.9.8 (no verified release/claim transition)`\n  - The active `:mem` store and REPL session survive the refusal intact.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Distributed Swarm Quick Start (DHT)\n\nWith `--index-store dht:<dir>` (or the `dht` subcommands), the REPL composes a `dao.space.dht` node over a durable, exclusively locked directory store.\n;; host-ffi.ss — the jolt.ffi host-owned surface for the GAMBIT target\n;; (P1b-stage1). On Chez these vars live in host/chez/java/ffi.ss, which the\n;; gambit boot manifest excludes — without this file every jolt.ffi name was an\n;; unbound global (the same shape the host-vars.ss degradation fixed for\n;; clojure.core). Stage-1 binds the memory/loader/errno surface the qualified\n;; tier backs (the adapter rows + the native bridge), implements what the\n;; stdlib/jolt/ffi.clj public functions call into, and binds EVERYTHING ELSE to\n;; a message-carrying refusal — nothing stays unbound, nothing pretends.\n;;\n;; Loaded after host-vars.ss, before the seed image (same rule: these bindings\n;; are the last word for the names the excluded ffi.ss owned). Requires the\n;; booted kernel (jolt-nil, def-var!, jolt-hash-map, make-pvec, ...) — this\n;; file is boot-world only; the sa-* tier it leans on also loads standalone\n;; under gambitcheck without it.\n\n(define (joltffi-public-error thunk)\n  (guard (e (#t (throw-jvm 'UnsupportedOperationException\n                            (or (condition-message e) \"jolt.ffi: native bridge failure\"))))\n    (thunk)))\n\n(define (joltffi-public-blocking-raise)\n  (joltffi-public-error sa-foreign-procedure-blocking-raise))\n\n(define (joltffi-public-procedure args ret name variadic capture?)\n  (let ((fp (joltffi-public-error\n             (lambda () ((if capture? joltffi-foreign-capture-procedure joltffi-foreign-procedure)\n                         args ret name variadic)))))\n    (lambda args\n      (joltffi-public-error (lambda () (apply fp args))))))\n\n(define (joltffi-str-arg what x)\n  (if (jolt-nil? x)\n      (throw-jvm 'IllegalArgumentException\n                 (string-append \"jolt.ffi: \" what \" must be a string, got nil\"))\n      (jolt-str-render-one x)))\n\n;; ---- emitted-text helpers the compiler's ordinary-call wrappers lean on -----\n;; The :string conversions invert per direction like the Chez pair; jolt truthy\n1493:deadhost:\n1506:emittedheads:\n1911:gambitgencheck:\n1915:\t    echo \"gambitgencheck: records-gambit.ss is current with the records files\"; \\\n1917:\t    echo \"gambitgencheck: host/gambit/records-gambit.ss is STALE — run 'make gambitgen'\" >&2; \\\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
